The animation loop runs at the browser's frame rate — usually 60 Hz, sometimes 120 Hz on modern displays, sometimes 30 Hz on a struggling phone. Animation logic must be frame-rate independent.
import * as THREE from 'three';
const clock = new THREE.Clock();
renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => {
const dt = clock.getDelta(); // seconds since last frame
cube.rotation.y += 1.0 * dt; // 1 radian per second, every device
renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
Never write cube.rotation.y += 0.01 — that's "0.01 radians per frame", which means twice as fast at 120 Hz and half as fast at 30 Hz. Always multiply by dt.
clock.getElapsedTime() returns total seconds since the clock started — great for sin-wave motion:
const t = clock.getElapsedTime();
cube.position.y = Math.sin(t * 2) * 0.5;

For simple effects, mutate properties each frame:
renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => {
const t = clock.getElapsedTime();
cube.rotation.x = t * 0.5;
cube.rotation.y = t * 0.7;
light.position.x = Math.cos(t) * 5;
light.position.z = Math.sin(t) * 5;
renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
Cheap, no library needed. Right answer for one-off effects.

For declarative "go from A to B over T seconds with easing", a tween library is cleaner:
npm install @tweenjs/tween.js
import { Tween, Easing, update as updateTweens } from '@tweenjs/tween.js';
new Tween(cube.position)
.to({ x: 5, y: 2, z: 0 }, 1500) // ms
.easing(Easing.Quadratic.InOut)
.start();
renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => {
updateTweens(); // tween.js needs a heartbeat
renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
GSAP is the heavyweight alternative — more features (timelines, scroll triggers, plugins) but a paid Club license for some features. For Three.js scenes, @tweenjs/tween.js is usually enough.

Authored animation (in Blender, Maya, etc.) comes back from GLTFLoader as gltf.animations — an array of AnimationClip objects. Play them with AnimationMixer:
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
const gltf = await new GLTFLoader().loadAsync('/character.glb');
scene.add(gltf.scene);
const mixer = new THREE.AnimationMixer(gltf.scene);
const idle = mixer.clipAction(THREE.AnimationClip.findByName(gltf.animations, 'Idle')).play();
const clock = new THREE.Clock();
renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => {
mixer.update(clock.getDelta());
renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
One AnimationMixer per character. It animates whatever properties the clip targets — bone transforms, morph influences, mesh visibility, even materials.

const action = mixer.clipAction(clip);
action.play();
action.stop();
action.reset();
action.setLoop(THREE.LoopOnce); // or LoopRepeat (default) / LoopPingPong
action.clampWhenFinished = true; // hold last frame after finishing
action.timeScale = 0.5; // half speed
action.weight = 0.7; // 0-1 contribution when blending
action.setEffectiveTimeScale(...);
action.setEffectiveWeight(...);

The bread-and-butter character pattern:
const walk = mixer.clipAction(walkClip);
const run = mixer.clipAction(runClip);
walk.play();
function startRun() {
run.reset().play();
walk.crossFadeTo(run, 0.3, true); // 0.3 s, warp time
}
function stopRun() {
walk.reset().play();
run.crossFadeTo(walk, 0.3, true);
}
crossFadeTo(other, duration, warp) — warp = true synchronises clip phase (a footstep mid-walk transitions to mid-run, not back to start).

walk.play();
run.play();
walk.weight = 0.7;
run.weight = 0.3; // 70% walk + 30% run = jog
Common for run/walk transitions driven by a movement-speed value.

mixer.addEventListener('finished', (e) => {
console.log('action finished', e.action);
});

A morph target is an alternate version of a mesh — same vertex count, different positions. Interpolating between morphs animates facial expressions, mouth shapes, body deformations.
A glTF with morph targets exposes mesh.morphTargetInfluences (array of 0-1 weights) and mesh.morphTargetDictionary (name → index).
const face = gltf.scene.getObjectByName('FaceMesh');
console.log(face.morphTargetDictionary);
// { mouthOpen: 0, smile: 1, eyesClosed: 2 }
face.morphTargetInfluences[face.morphTargetDictionary.smile] = 1.0;
face.morphTargetInfluences[face.morphTargetDictionary.mouthOpen] = 0.3;
Animate manually or via AnimationMixer (glTF animations target morph influences automatically when authored to).

A skinned mesh deforms based on a skeleton (a hierarchy of bones). Each vertex is weighted against up to 4 bones; transforming bones deforms the surface.
glTF embeds skin + skeleton + animation clips together; GLTFLoader reconstructs them as THREE.SkinnedMesh + THREE.Skeleton.
gltf.scene.traverse((c) => {
if (c.isSkinnedMesh) {
scene.add(new THREE.SkeletonHelper(c)); // visualise bones for debug
}
});
Skinned animation is what makes characters walk, fight, dance. Authoring happens in Blender / Maya / Houdini; Three.js mostly plays back.

Three.js has CCDIKSolver (Cyclic Coordinate Descent) for runtime IK — useful for "foot stays planted" or "hand reaches at a target":
import { CCDIKSolver } from 'three/addons/animation/CCDIKSolver.js';
const ikSolver = new CCDIKSolver(skinnedMesh, iks); // iks: bone chain config
renderer.setAnimationLoop(() => {
mixer.update(dt);
ikSolver.update();
renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
For more advanced IK (FABRIK, multi-effector, retargeting), use three-ik or a runtime like Mixamo + retarget tools.

A clip authored for one skeleton plays on a different skeleton via the SkeletonUtils.retargetClip helper:
import { SkeletonUtils } from 'three/addons/utils/SkeletonUtils.js';
const retargeted = SkeletonUtils.retargetClip(
targetSkeletonRoot, sourceSkeletonRoot, clip, { hip: 'mixamorigHips' }
);
Useful for: applying Mixamo animations to your own characters; reusing one walking animation across many character models.
